<BaseLayout title="Transmission & Clutch" description="T56 Magnum XL specs and ratios, pilot bushing, shifter relocation, Z33 and automatic options, and the Wilwood clutch master fix for LS Genesis Coupe swaps.">
  <h1>Transmission &amp; Clutch</h1>
  <p>
    The Gen Coupe accepts both manual and automatic LS transmissions with the correct adapter, crossmember, and driveshaft.
    Common choices are the T56 Magnum XL (manual) and 4L60E / 4L80E / 6L80E (automatic).
  </p>

  <h2>T56 Magnum XL</h2>
  <p>
    Requires a custom pilot bushing since it uses a Ford input shaft — <strong>unless</strong> you get the GM shaft.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Pilot bushing:</strong> McLeod Racing 8-1094-2 — Oilite, GM except Pontiac, 1.094" OD x .672" ID</li>
    <li><strong>Clutch alignment tool:</strong> <a href="https://www.americanmuscle.com/ram-26-spline-clutch-alignment-tool.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RAM 26-spline clutch alignment tool</a></li>
  </ul>
  <div class="figs">
    <Figure src="mcleod-pilot-bushing-mrr-8-1094-2.jpg" alt="McLeod 8-1094-2 pilot bushing in bag" caption="McLeod 8-1094-2 pilot bushing." />
    <Figure src="ram-clutch-alignment-tool.jpg" alt="RAM 26-spline clutch alignment tool listing" caption="RAM 26-spline alignment tool." />
  </div>
  <h3>Magnum XL (GM) Specs</h3>
  <table>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Torque rating</td><td>700 lb-ft, 7,800 rpm max</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Ratios (TUET18131)</td><td>2.66 / 1.78 / 1.30 / 1.00 / 0.80 / 0.63, rev 2.90</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Ratios (TUET18132)</td><td>2.97 / 2.10 / 1.46 / 1.00 / 0.80 / 0.63, rev 2.90</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Input / output</td><td>26-spline input (6.5" long) / 31-spline slip yoke</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Electrical</td><td>Electronic speedo output, reverse lockout solenoid, reverse light switch</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Fluid</td><td>3.66 qt: Tremec HP-MTF, Synchromesh, or Dexron III ATF</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Weight</td><td>~150 lb dry</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>The 2.66 first gear suits higher-power and boosted cars; the 2.97 gives a stronger launch with a mild N/A engine.</p>

  <h3>Shifter</h3>
  <p>
    The T56 shifter lands in a different spot than the stock Genesis shifter. Expect to notch the tunnel or shifter plate. ISR sells a T56 Genesis shifter relocation kit (IS-GNLS-SHFT), and one LS1 build used a Hurst short shifter, GTO shifter cup, and a shifter extension.
  </p>

  <h2>Other Transmission Options</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>TR6060</strong>: supported by Sikky's trans mount and driveshaft options.</li>
    <li><strong>Keep a Nissan Z33/Z34 6-speed</strong>: LOJ's Z33 kit includes an adapter plate, Clutchmasters billet flywheel (350Z/G35 clutch), mini starter, and shortened 350Z-style linkage.</li>
    <li><strong>Automatics (4L80E, 6L80/6L90)</strong>: LOJ sells trans mounts for these. Automatic control needs a Terminator X Max or a separate controller. LOJ warns that GM transmissions may require tunnel modification.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Reverse lights and speedo for the T56 are covered under <a href="/guides/wiring#manual-reverse">Wiring</a> and <a href="/guides/ecu#speedo">ECUs &amp; Tuning</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="clutch-master">Clutch Master Cylinder</h2>
  <p>You'll need a Wilwood master cylinder with an adapter from ISR. Two small issues I found:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Add a thin rubber washer behind the clutch pedal pin, and file down the cylinder mounting portion so it sits flat on the washer and pedal.</li>
    <li>Cut the rubber dust boot that comes with the Wilwood master cylinder so it fits behind the Wilwood adapter. This helps preserve the cylinder's life.</li>
  </ol>
  <Figure src="clutch-master-cylinder-pin.jpg" alt="Clutch pedal pin and Wilwood master cylinder mount" caption="Clutch pedal pin and master cylinder mount." />

